# Describing a list of numbers
# The mean, the median, the spread and a histogram of eight exam scores, all exact.
# Eight scores. A list is written in brackets:
m:algebra scores = [52, 61, 61, 68, 70, 75, 83, 90]
# The centre of the data: the mean is the sum divided by the count, the median is the middle value, the mode the most frequent.
m:algebra mean(scores)
m:algebra median(scores)
m:algebra mode(scores)
# The spread: the sample variance divides by n - 1, the population one by n. The standard deviation is the square root, exact when the variance is a perfect square.
m:algebra var(scores)
m:algebra std(scores)
m:algebra pvar(scores)
# Quartiles cut the sorted data in four, the interquartile range is the middle half:
m:algebra quantile(scores, 1/4)
m:algebra quantile(scores, 3/4)
m:algebra iqr(scores)
# How many scores are in each of four equal bins between the smallest and the largest:
m:algebra histogram(scores, 4)
# A z-score says how many standard deviations a value is from the mean:
m:algebra zscore(90, mean(scores), std(scores))
